\end{asy}

Its diagonals have length $200$, so its area is $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot200\cdot200=\boxed{20000}$.

Such a triangle has two equal sides $a$ and a different third side $b$, with $1\le a,b\le10$ and $b\ne a$; the triangle inequality requires only $b<2a$ (the other inequality $a<a+b$ is automatic). For each $a$ count the $b$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a=1$: $b<2$, $b\ne1$: none;
\item $a=2,3,4,5$: $b=1,\dots,2a-1$ except $a$: $2,4,6,8$ choices;
\item $a=6,\dots,10$: $b=1,\dots,10$ except $a$: $9$ choices each.
\end{itemize}
Different pairs $(a,b)$ give different triangles. The total is $2+4+6+8+5\cdot9=\boxed{65}$, choice (1).

