The sketch shows a triangle with equal sides $a$ and base $b$; the triangle inequality only requires $b<a+a$.

question:
Albert, Bertha and Carlos play rounds of a game with a fair coin. In a round the players flip in a fixed order, cycling through the three players, and the round stops as soon as someone flips heads; that player wins the round. In the first round the order is Albert, Bertha, Carlos. In each later round, the winner of the previous round goes last and the other two keep their relative order. (For example, if Albert wins the first round, the second round goes Bertha, Carlos, Albert; if Bertha then wins, the third round goes Carlos, Albert, Bertha.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find the probability that Albert wins the third round.
\item[(b)] Let $p_n$ be the probability that Albert wins round $n$. Prove that $\left|p_{2018}-\dfrac{1}{3}\right|<10^{-300}$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
The three square roots are the distances between the points $P=(a,b)$, $Q=(c,d)$, $R=(e,f)$, and $g$ is half their sum.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
import graph;
pair P1=(1,1),P2=(6,2),P3=(2.5,5);
draw((-0.5,0)--(7.5,0),Arrow);draw((0,-0.5)--(0,6),Arrow);
label("$x$",(7.5,0),S);label("$y$",(0,6),W);
draw(P1--P2--P3--cycle,linewidth(0.9));dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(P3);
label("$(a,b)$",P1,SW);label("$(c,d)$",P2,E);label("$(e,f)$",P3,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

If $P,Q,R$ form a triangle, $g$ is its semiperimeter and the expression is, by Heron's formula, its area $K$. By the shoelace formula
\[
K=\dfrac{1}{2}\left|a(d-f)+c(f-b)+e(b-d)\right| ,
\]
which is rational since $a,\dots,f$ are.

If the points are collinear (or two coincide), the largest distance is the sum of the other two, so $g$ equals the largest distance and one factor is $0$; the expression is $0$, again rational (and the shoelace value is also $0$). In every case the expression is rational. $\blacksquare$

Everything happens in the plane $\mathbf{P}_1$, which contains $\ell$; the plane $\mathbf{P}_2$ plays no role. Tangency puts the centres on the radii: $O_2$ is the midpoint of $O_1T_1$, so $O_1O_2=1$ in the direction of $T_1$, and $O_3$ lies beyond $T_2$ on ray $O_1T_2$ with $O_1O_3=3$.

The direction $\overrightarrow{T_1O_1}$ makes $30^\circ$ with $\ell$, so the direction $\overrightarrow{O_1O_2}$ also makes $30^\circ$ with $\ell$ (it is the opposite direction), and $\overrightarrow{O_1O_3}$ is perpendicular to $\ell$. Hence the angle $\theta=\angle O_2O_1O_3$ is $90^\circ-30^\circ=60^\circ$ or $90^\circ+30^\circ=120^\circ$, depending on which side of $\ell$ the point $O_3$ is taken. By the law of cosines,
\[
O_2O_3^2=1^2+3^2-2\cdot1\cdot3\cos\theta=10-6\cos\theta ,
\]
which is $7$ for $\theta=60^\circ$ and $13$ for $\theta=120^\circ$. The configuration with $\theta=60^\circ$ is shown:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O1=(0,2.6);
real c=Cos(30),sn=Sin(30);
pair O2=O1+(-c,sn),T1=O1+2*(-c,sn),O3=O1+(0,3),T2=O1+(0,2);
pair H=O1+(2.6/sn)*(c,-sn);
draw((-3.6,0)--(5.2,0),linewidth(0.8));label("$\ell$",(5.2,0),E);
draw(circle(O1,2),linewidth(0.9));draw(circle(O2,1),blue);draw(circle(O3,1),red);
draw(T1--H,dashed);draw(O1--T2,dashed);
draw(O2--O3,heavygreen+linewidth(1));
draw(arc(H,1.1,150,180));label("$30^\circ$",H+2.0*dir(165));
dot(O1);dot(O2);dot(O3);dot(T1);dot(T2);
label("$O_1$",O1,dir(15));label("$O_2$",O2,dir(250));label("$O_3$",O3,E);label("$T_1$",T1,W);label("$T_2$",T2+0.25*dir(-40),dir(-40));
label("$\sqrt7$",(-1.5,4.95),heavygreen);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The minimum is $\sqrt7$, so $m=\boxed{7}$.

Work from the inside out, using that $9^{\text{odd}}$ ends in $9$ and $9^{\text{even}}$ ends in $1$; $2019$ ends in $9$, so $u(2019)=9$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $2019\,u(2019)=18171$ is odd, so $u\left(2019^{18171}\right)=9$, and $u\left(9^9\right)=9$.
\item $u\left(2019^{2018}\right)=1$, so $u\left(1^{2019}\right)=1$.
\end{itemize}
The exponent is $9\times1=9$, and $u\left(2019^9\right)=\boxed{9}$.

We need $2019^{m}$ modulo $100$, i.e.\ $19^m\bmod100$. Now $19^2=361\equiv61$, $19^4\equiv61^2\equiv21$, $19^5\equiv21\cdot19=399\equiv99\equiv-1$, so $19^{10}\equiv1\pmod{100}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $2019^{2019}\equiv19^{9}\equiv19^{-1}\equiv79\pmod{100}$ (as $19\cdot79=1501$). So $t\left(2019^{2019}\right)=7$ and $u(7)=7$.
\item $u\left(2019^{2018}\right)=1$, $t(1)=0$, and $2019^0=1$.
\end{itemize}
The exponent is $7\times1=7$, and $2019^7\equiv19^7\equiv19^5\cdot19^2\equiv-61\equiv39\pmod{100}$. The tens digit is $\boxed{3}$.

In the order $W,X,Y,Z$, the vertices $W$ and $Y$ are opposite, so $WY$ is a diagonal. (They need not be adjacent, which is why ``$W$ and $Y$ do not form a side'' is no obstacle.) The centre is the midpoint $M=(3,4)$, and $\overrightarrow{MW}=(-1,-3)$. The other two vertices are obtained by rotating this vector through $\pm90^\circ$: $(3,-1)$ and $(-3,1)$. So $X$ and $Z$ are $(6,3)$ and $(0,5)$ in some order.

\begin{asy}
