Hence
\[
\angle BPC=180^\circ-\angle ABC-\angle ACB+35^\circ=\angle BAC+35^\circ,
\]
using the angle sum of triangle $ABC$. So $\angle BPC-\angle BAC=\boxed{35^\circ}$, whatever the shape of the triangle.

Let $R$ be the unit square $[0,1]^2$ and $X=(x,y)$ an interior point.

\textbf{The rays must go to the four corners.} Each of the $n$ regions is a triangle with a vertex at $X$; a region containing a corner of the square in the interior of its boundary arc would not be a triangle. So four of the rays go to the corners, and they split $R$ into four triangles on the sides with areas $\dfrac {x}{2},\dfrac{1-x}{2},\dfrac {y}{2},\dfrac{1-y}{2}$ (base $1$, height the distance from $X$ to that side). The sketch shows the case $n=10$, $X=\left(\dfrac{2}{5},\dfrac{3}{5}\right)$: the solid rays go to the corners and the dashed rays cut the four side triangles into $2,3,3,2$ equal parts.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real u=5; pair X=(0.4u,0.6u);
pair[] K={(0,0),(u,0),(u,u),(0,u)};
filldraw((0,0)--(u,0)--(u,u)--(0,u)--cycle,white,linewidth(0.9));
for(int i=0;i<4;++i) draw(X--K[i],linewidth(0.8));
pair[] Pp={(0,0.5u),(u,u/3),(u,2u/3),(u/3,0),(2u/3,0),(0.5u,u)};
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) draw(X--Pp[i],blue+dashed);
dot(X);label("$X$",X+0.62*dir(103));
label("$x$",(0.2u,0.6u),N);label("$1-x$",(3.9,0.6u),S);
draw(X--(0,0.6u),dotted);draw(X--(u,0.6u),dotted);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}


\textbf{Counting.} The remaining rays split each of these four triangles into triangles with a common height, so each triangle on a side is cut into some number $m_i\ge1$ of pieces of area $\dfrac{1}{n}$. Hence $\dfrac {x}{2}=\dfrac{m_1}{n}$ and $\dfrac{1-x}{2}=\dfrac{m_2}{n}$, so $x=\dfrac{2m_1}{n}$, and similarly for $y$. Conversely, if $x$ and $y$ are multiples of $\dfrac{2}{n}$ strictly between $0$ and $1$, dividing each side into equal parts gives $n$ triangles of area $\dfrac{1}{n}$ (then $m_1+m_2+m_3+m_4=\dfrac {n}{2}+\dfrac {n}{2}=n$).

So the $100$-ray partitional points are those with $x,y\in\left\{\dfrac{1}{50},\dots,\dfrac{49}{50}\right\}$: $49^2=2401$ points. The $60$-ray partitional points have coordinates that are multiples of $\dfrac{1}{30}$. A number that is a multiple of both $\dfrac{1}{50}$ and $\dfrac{1}{30}$ is a multiple of $\dfrac{1}{10}$ (if $\dfrac {p}{50}=\dfrac {q}{30}$ then $3p=5q$, so $5\mid p$), and there are $9^2=81$ points with both coordinates in $\left\{\dfrac{1}{10},\dots,\dfrac{9}{10}\right\}$. The answer is $2401-81=\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 2320}$.

A radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent, so $OA\perp AB$ and $OC\perp CB$. Triangles $OAB$ and $OCB$ are congruent (hypotenuse $OB$ common, $OA=OC$), so $OB$ bisects $\angle ABC$ and $\angle ABO=60^\circ$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=8*sqrt(3);
pair O=(0,0),B=(-16,0),A=r*dir(150),C=r*dir(210);
draw(circle(O,r),linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--B--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--A,dashed);draw(O--C,dashed);draw(O--B,dashed);
pair u=unit(B-A),w=unit(O-A);draw(A+1.4*u--A+1.4*u+1.4*w--A+1.4*w);
pair u2=unit(B-C),w2=unit(O-C);draw(C+1.4*u2--C+1.4*u2+1.4*w2--C+1.4*w2);
draw(arc(B,1.4,-60,60));label("$\angle ABC=120^\circ$",(-16,-15));
dot(O);
label("$O$",O,E);label("$A$",A,dir(150));label("$C$",C,dir(210));label("$B$",B,W);
label("$8$",(A+B)/2,NW);label("$r$",(O+A)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

In the right triangle $OAB$, $r=OA=AB\tan60^\circ=8\sqrt3$. The area of the circle is $\pi r^2=\boxed{192\pi}$.

Let $\angle BAC=x$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(0,6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real x=180/7;
pair A=(0,0),B=3*dir(-90-x/2),C=3*dir(-90+x/2);
real t=abs(B-C);
pair D=t*dir(-90+x/2),Ep=2*t*Cos(x)*dir(-90-x/2);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(D--Ep--C,blue+linewidth(0.8));
void tk(pair P,pair Q){pair m=(P+Q)/2,v=0.12*unit(rotate(90)*(Q-P));draw(m-v--m+v,linewidth(0.7));}
tk(A,D);tk(D,Ep);tk(Ep,C);tk(C,B);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,SW);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,E);label("$E$",Ep,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{itemize}
\item $AD=DE$: in triangle $ADE$, $\angle DEA=\angle DAE=x$, so the exterior angle $\angle EDC=2x$.
\item $DE=EC$: in triangle $DEC$, $\angle ECD=\angle EDC=2x$.
\item In triangle $AEC$, the exterior angle at $E$ is $\angle CEB=\angle EAC+\angle ECA=x+2x=3x$.
\item $EC=CB$: in triangle $ECB$, $\angle CBE=\angle CEB=3x$.
\end{itemize}
Since $AB=AC$, also $\angle ACB=\angle ABC=3x$. The angle sum of triangle $ABC$ gives $x+3x+3x=180^\circ$, so $\angle BAC=\boxed{\left(\dfrac{180}{7}\right)^\circ}\approx25.7^\circ$.

Let $\theta$ be the angle between the hands. By the law of cosines the distance between the tips is
\[
d=\sqrt{3^2+4^2-2\cdot3\cdot4\cos\theta}=\sqrt{25-24\cos\theta}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,4.6),linewidth(0.9));
for(int k=0;k<12;++k) draw(4.3*dir(90-30*k)--4.6*dir(90-30*k));
pair H=3*dir(70),M=4*dir(-10);
draw(O--H,linewidth(1.6));draw(O--M,linewidth(1));
draw(H--M,red+dashed);
draw(arc(O,0.9,-10,70));label("$\theta$",1.3*dir(30));
dot(O);
label("$3$",H/2,W);label("$4$",M/2,S);label("$d$",(H+M)/2,NE,red);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

As the angle from the hour hand to the minute hand goes once round, from $0^\circ$ to $360^\circ$, $d$ increases from $1$ to $7$ (at $180^\circ$) and decreases back to $1$. So in each full turn the integer values $2,3,4,5,6$ occur twice each and $1$ and $7$ once each: $12$ times.

The minute hand gains $330^\circ$ per hour on the hour hand, so in the $12$ hours it gains $12\cdot330^\circ=11\cdot360^\circ$: eleven full turns of the relative angle. At $1{:}00$ (start and end) the angle is $30^\circ$ and $d^2=25-12\sqrt3$ is not an integer, so no occurrence is double-counted at the endpoints. The answer is $11\cdot12=\boxed{132}$.
